{# THE PLAIN-TEXT PART, AND IT IS WRITTEN FIRST ON PURPOSE — same order and same reason as
   `verify_email.txt.twig`.

   A `multipart/alternative` message with no text part scores badly with every spam filter worth the
   name, because the text part is the version a filter can read without executing anything. That is
   deliverability work (PRD validation #3), not a courtesy — and it matters more here than on any
   other message the application sends, because this one is the *only* way back into an account.
   A reset mail that lands in spam is an account that cannot be recovered.

   It must therefore say the *same* thing as the HTML part rather than being a stub: same link, same
   deadline, same reassurance.

   Whitespace control matters here in a way it does not in an HTML template: what is between the tags
   is literally what lands in the mailbox. The file's exact line breaks are the layout. #}
Reset your muzbar password

Somebody asked to reset the password for the muzbar account with this address:

    {{ recipientEmail }}

Open the link below to choose a new password:

{{ resetUrl }}

{# The second argument to `date` pins the timezone. Without it Twig renders in `twig.date.timezone`
   — PHP's default, i.e. whatever the container happens to be set to — so the same instant would
   print differently in the worker and in a test, and the word "UTC" beside it would eventually be a
   lie. The Clock contract guarantees the value *is* UTC; this makes the rendering say so truthfully
   rather than by luck.

   `lifetime` is a phrase built by `TwigPasswordResetMailer` from
   `PasswordResetRequest::LIFETIME_SECONDS`, never typed here. Both forms are printed on purpose: the
   duration is what someone reading immediately acts on, and the absolute instant is what someone
   opening the mail hours later needs in order to understand why the link no longer works. #}
The link can be used once and expires in {{ lifetime }}, at {{ expiresAt|date('Y-m-d H:i', 'UTC') }} UTC.
If it has already expired, ask for a new one from the sign-in page.

{# The line the whole message is built around. Somebody who did not do this is, at the moment they
   read the subject, worried — and the honest, calming and *accurate* thing to tell them is that
   nothing has happened yet and nothing will unless they act. It is also why the mail can afford to
   be as plain as it is: no alarm, no "secure your account now", no link to anything but the reset
   form. Note what is absent — no mention of the current password, no username, no hint about the
   account beyond the address above, and obviously no credential of any kind. #}
If you did not request this, you can ignore this email — your password has not been changed.

--
muzbar
